AB  - Effectively transferring image-level scene understanding from Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) to instance-level Human-Object Interaction (HOI) detection remains a critical bottleneck for safety-critical traffic applications. Existing knowledge distillation methods rely on fixed, manually-engineered prompts that fail to guarantee the quality of generated training data. To overcome this, we propose the T-HOI-BPO framework (Traffic HOI Detection with Bayesian Prompt Optimization and Cross-level Distillation), which formalizes prompt engineering as a Bayesian optimization problem, establishing a closed-loop pipeline where a teacher MLLM’s prompts are iteratively refined based on student model feedback. We further design domain-specific components called Traffic-aware Visual-Linguistic Translator (T-VLT) and a Traffic Interaction Cognition Network (T-ICN) which along with traffic safety-oriented distillation losses to effectively bridge the representation gap between image-level understanding and instance-level interaction detection. The distilled knowledge is transferred to T-CarGPT, a lightweight student model built on YOLOv9, designed for real-time in-vehicle deployment. Extensive experiments demonstrate that T-CarGPT, trained exclusively on BPO-optimized synthetic data, achieves a 24.8% mAP, 89.2% safety accuracy, and 91.7% rule compliance accuracy, outperforming both fully-supervised baselines and fixed-prompt alternatives while maintaining an inference latency of ∼25 ms. This work provides a systematic methodology for unlocking MLLM knowledge for practical traffic HOI detection and demonstrates its effectiveness within the intelligent transportation domain.
