AB  - Extracting structured information from Piping and Instrumentation Diagrams (P&IDs) is a foundational requirement for digital twins, yet the field faces a deadlock: supervised deep learning methods depend on large, annotated datasets that are prohibitively expensive to generate for proprietary legacy assets. While Vision-Language Models (VLMs) offer a potential training-free alternative, their reliability on high-entropy engineering schematics remains unverified. This paper proposes a training-free framework to overcome these issues by combining computer vision-based preprocessing with VLM few-shot capabilities. To address the challenge of high information density in engineering drawings, our approach employs a density-based segmentation strategy that partitions diagrams into manageable complexity windows, followed by geometric line reconstruction and few-shot VLM prompting to extract equipment and connectivity. Validated on real industrial datasets, the framework achieves 83% accuracy in symbol association and 82% accuracy in line association, significantly outperforming state-of-the-art supervised methods (73%) in a few-shot setting, without requiring any training data. Crucially, our work provides empirical evidence towards characterizing the limits of foundation models in this domain, identifying that a total entropy greater than 2×106 and edge complexity greater than 0.5 serve as important thresholds for inference failure. This work not only delivers a practical solution for digitizing legacy assets but also establishes the boundary conditions for the effective deployment of generative AI in engineering informatics.
