AB  - Hazard source identification in metro operation and maintenance faces challenges like high data dimensionality, data heterogeneity and difficult data structuring. Existing general knowledge graphs fail to fully consider domain - specific business logic, leading to low identification accuracy and frequent catastrophic incidents. To solve this, this paper develops a new identification method based on the dual knowledge self -verification large language model, which boosts accuracy and enables efficient targeted control. It establishes a three - dimensional “root cause – hazard source - hazardous event” framework, integrates In - Context Learning with Chain of Thought for reasoning, uses Transformer multi - head self - attention to extract knowledge triplets, applies dual verification to build hierarchical causal knowledge graphs, and creates a risk quantification model. Validation with metro data shows domain knowledge extraction has an 86.6% F1 score (18.1% higher than UIE model). Targeting the critical “inadequate management and maintenance” node (78.4% of cumulative disaster severity weight) cuts accident rate by 16.66%. This research enables a shift from passive to proactive metro disaster risk control.
