AB  - Efficient deployment of language models often relies on weight pruning, but compression is usually judged by perplexity or task accuracy. These aggregate metrics do not reveal whether pruning preserves the internal feature structure on which interpretability analyses depend. We study pruning as a representation-level transformation, using Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) as geometric feature probes: our contribution is not a new SAE architecture but a compression-aware diagnostic use of SAE dictionaries that asks whether dense-model feature directions remain geometrically recoverable after pruning. Across Gemma 3 1B, Gemma 2 2B, and Llama 3.2 1B, we compare magnitude pruning and Wanda from 0% to 60% sparsity, extend the study with exploratory probes of further compression families (Wanda 2:4, an MLP-channel pilot, and int8/int4 quantization), and evaluate feature matching, transferability, firing-rate fragility, and local ablation, with statistical uncertainty reported throughout. Three results stand out. First, low-firing-rate SAE features show higher geometric survival under pruning than high-firing-rate features, making firing rate a useful pre-pruning indicator of fragility. Second, Wanda preserves SAE feature geometry substantially better than magnitude pruning at matched sparsity, and a fixed-dictionary control that removes SAE retraining variability reproduces the same ordering. Third, geometric survival does not predict local functional effect: under a reconstruction-error-free single-feature ablation with firing rate controlled, survival and ablation sensitivity are dissociated. All claims are associational. These findings suggest that compression should be evaluated not only by output-level performance, but also by feature-level preservation, and that SAE-based diagnostics offer a practical tool for monitoring interpretability under pruning.
ER  - 

