AB  - Accurate estimation of clothing insulation levels is critical for effective thermal-comfort management and occupant-centric HVAC control in smart buildings. Existing approaches either rely on static seasonal assumptions, on environmental regressions that ignore individual variability, or on wearable sensors and RGB cameras that are intrusive and raise significant privacy concerns. To address this gap, this study proposes a novel framework employing a multimodal large language model (MLLM) fine-tuned on thermal imagery for clothing insulation classification. We first introduce ThermalCloth500, a dataset containing 500 thermal images of individuals in diverse indoor and outdoor environments, labeled into three insulation categories (light, medium, and heavy). Three classification models, including a baseline Vision Transformer (ViT-Base/16), a zero-shot Gemini-2.0-flash, and a fine-tuned Gemini-2.0-flash were developed and systematically evaluated. Results demonstrated that the fine-tuned Gemini model substantially outperformed the ViT baseline and zero-shot Gemini, achieving 94% accuracy and showing robustness against partial visual occlusion. Occlusion-sensitivity and saliency analyses further highlighted the fine-tuned Gemini’s superior ability to reason from limited thermal cues compared to the transformer-based vision baseline. Despite the higher inference latency of MLLMs, their semantic reasoning capabilities significantly enhanced both classification accuracy and interpretability. This research contributes the first thermal-image dataset dedicated to clothing insulation classification, validates the effectiveness of fine-tuned MLLMs in this context, and highlights pathways for future work focused on edge optimization and hybrid modeling approaches.
