AB  - Reordering input sentences in large language models (LLMs) often leads to semantic inconsistencies in the output, as the intended meaning of the input can change. Permutation bias arises when the overall meaning of the input remains unchanged, yet the output still differs-this issue is particularly evident in tasks involving multiple-choice questions and lists of facts. Recent studies have confirmed that permutation bias is prevalent across various LLMs and types of tasks. To address this problem, we propose the Average Attention Infer module, which leverages the attention mechanism to reduce permutation bias by aligning attention weights across different input permutations. We conducted experiments to demonstrate the effectiveness of our module in mitigating permutation bias compared to strong baselines. Furthermore, our findings confirm that LLMs retain their language processing capabilities after undergoing debiasing, making our module adaptable to other tasks. To further enhance computational efficiency, we provide methods for selecting optimal layers and input permutations. Comparative experiments demonstrate that our method comprehensively outperforms SOTA baselines under conditions of smaller models and lower cost. The code for our work is publicly available. We hope that our research will contribute to fundamentally eliminating permutation bias in LLMs.
ER  - 

