AB  - Urban noise increasingly threatens public health and quality of life in rapidly urbanizing megacities. Traditional decibel‐based metrics and flat‐terrain mitigation strategies fail to capture residents’ subjective perceptions or address the intricate sound propagation created by complex and vertically expanding landscapes. Yet existing research seldom integrates three‐dimensional morphology, demographic heterogeneity, and emotional intensity into a unified, nonlinear framework. To address this, we apply LLM‐based sentiment analysis to extract complaint intensity and combine these scores with normalized complaint rates in an XGBoost model interpreted via SHAP values, thereby capturing how building–terrain interplay, façade alignment, vegetation patterns, and age composition jointly drive noise grievances. Our results reveal that exceeding a critical building‐height‐density threshold yields a 12 % reduction in complaints, while façade misalignment amplifies them by up to 10 %. Dense vegetative corridors within narrow canyons further enhance noise mitigation—cutting complaints by about 7 %—and elderly‐dominated neighborhoods derive two to three times greater benefit from barrier and greenery interventions than younger areas. Moreover, entertainment POIs chiefly drive social noise complaints, barrier metrics are key for transportation noise, and construction complaints reflect mixed spatial influences. We advocate a dynamic, data‐driven governance paradigm that links real‐time digital complaint platforms with AI‐powered sensitivity maps to calibrate evidence‐based, demographic‐tailored design and mitigation strategies.
