AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) offer a powerful analytical tool to perform various natural language processing tasks. However, their potential capabilities have not been widely perceived and employed by tourism and hospitality (T&H) researchers due to validity and reliability risks from technology constraints (e.g., limited domain-specific expertise, hallucination, novelty). This study validated pre-trained LLMs’ capabilities in two important classification tasks in T&H—sentiment analysis and thematic classification—while investigating performance variations under different conditions of input length, prompt complexity, and model usability. Furthermore, this study applied three prompting techniques to extract latent LLM capabilities for improving performance. These investigations were performed based on technology affordance and metacognitive theories. Results support the validity and reliability of LLM-generated outputs for both classification tasks and confirm that prompting techniques effectively boost classification accuracy in T&H contexts. These evidence-based insights into LLM applications guide T&H researchers in effectively incorporating LLMs into their future research.
