AB  - Conventional optical and soil-based sensing methods have limited robustness for crop water stress detection under complex field conditions, while acoustic emission (AE) based monitoring is constrained by sparse samples and environmental noise. To address this limitation, this study proposes an artificial intelligence-based framework integrating environment-conditioned spectrogram augmentation and multi-level regional fusion for cotton water stress monitoring. Distributed AE sensors were deployed at the root, stem, and leaf positions to collect acoustic signals under different irrigation conditions. F-bank and G-bank spectrograms were constructed to characterize cavitation-related time-frequency features. Soil moisture, transpiration rate, and environmental parameters were converted into structured conditional information and encoded using a lightweight Transformer. The encoded conditions were then integrated into a conditional generative adversarial network (CGAN) for spectrogram augmentation. Based on the augmented dataset, a multi-organ hierarchical attention network (MO-HAN) was developed for frame-level feature extraction, plant-level multi-organ fusion, and regional-level consistency verification. Five-fold cross-validation showed that the single-sensor model achieved 62.5 ± 2.8% accuracy, which increased to 78.2± 2.1% after multi-organ fusion and further improved to 83.7± 1.9% after regional verification (p < 0.05). Compared with direct numerical conditioning, the proposed framework improved macro-averaged F1 score (Macro-F1) by 5.4% and early-stress F1 by 6.1%. Stable convergence of training and validation losses further demonstrated good generalization under limited samples. These results indicate that environment-conditioned spectrogram augmentation improves the robustness of AE-based monitoring, while multi-level regional fusion enhances monitoring consistency at field scale, providing a scalable and practical solution for precision irrigation and smart agricultural sensing.
