AB  - While large language models (LLMs) are transforming engineering through enhanced control capabilities and decision support, their evolving complexity as dynamical systems requires careful regulation. This duality reflects a reciprocal link: prompts aid control design, while control theory guides prompts to achieve goals efficiently. In this study, we frame the interplay between LLMs and control as a bidirectional yet fundamentally asymmetric continuum, where each direction serves a unique function. First, we investigate how LLMs can advance control in two capacities: directly, by assisting in controller design and synthesis, and indirectly, by augmenting research workflows. Second, we examine how control concepts help LLMs steer outputs away from undesired meanings, improving reachability and alignment via controllable text generation. Third, we explore deeper integration by treating LLMs as dynamic systems in a state–space framework, linking internal representations to external control loops. Finally, we outline key challenges and future trends to understand LLM behavior and develop interpretable and controllable models that are as trustworthy and robust as their electromechanical counterparts, thereby ensuring they continue to support and safeguard society.
