AB  - Public Telegram channels are frequently exploited for cybercrime, creating a need for scalable monitoring. Such monitoring faces three gaps: analyst-validated labels are scarce, analysts must be able to audit why a channel is flagged, and channel content is short, noisy, multilingual, and evolves quickly. This paper presents TACTIC (Topic-guided Artificial Intelligence for Cybercriminal Telegram Identification and Classification), a novel application of artificial intelligence to Open Source Intelligence (OSINT) that addresses these three gaps. The implemented artificial intelligence pipeline uses a single, globally tuned BERTopic model, based on Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT), to summarize short multilingual channel content into compact topic cues over ten-message micro-batches. Subsequently, a Generative Pre-trained Transformer - Open Source Software (GPT-OSS) Large Language Model (LLM) analyzes these summaries alongside sampled channel messages to identify illicit activity on the platform, requiring no task-specific training labels and accompanying every decision with an auditable rationale and cited evidence. Experiments on live-collected and historical Telegram traffic demonstrate that TACTIC achieves high-quality detection and taxonomy assignment with minimal supervision, flagging 84.3% (273/324) of the channels in the curated DarkGram benchmark while sustaining 570–960 channels per hour on a single graphics processing unit.
