AB  - Alzheimer’s Disease, the most prevalent neurodegenerative disorder worldwide, presents substantial diagnostic challenges that necessitate the integration of multimodal data. This study introduces CogniVision-T5, a multimodal Large Language Model architecture designed for automated diagnostic report generation in Alzheimer’s Disease. Our framework integrates 3D structural neuroimaging with comprehensive clinical data using a tailored architecture where Magnetic Resonance Imaging volumes are processed by a 3D EVA-ViT encoder refined through a Q-Former and an enhanced multimodal projection layer. Clinical text is tokenized and encoded, then visual and clinical representations are concatenated and decoded by a T5 model to produce coherent diagnostic narratives. A key contribution of this work is the creation of a synthetic diagnostic report corpus for model training and evaluation, developed from the Alzheimer’s Disease Neuroimaging Initiative dataset. The model achieves high performance across established natural language generation metrics, with a BERTScore of 0.9114, ROUGE-L of 0.4892, and competitive BLEU scores with a BLEU-4 of 0.1590 and METEOR score of 0.4607. Internal ablation studies validated the model’s architecture and effectiveness, while external comparisons demonstrated that our framework outperforms general-purpose Large Language Models in generating clinically relevant reports. These results highlight the framework’s ability to produce consistent, clinically useful diagnostic summaries, positioning it as a supportive tool in the assessment and management of Alzheimer’s Disease. The full codebase is publicly accessible at: CogniVision-T5
