AB  - The frequent occurrence of extreme weather events significantly increases the risk of faults in photovoltaic (PV) microgrids, challenging the stability of power systems. Traditional warning methods focus on post-diagnosis or in-process alerts, lacking the ability to intervene during the early fault incubation stage. This paper proposes a proactive fault warning framework based on Scenario Analysis Theory, integrating Large Language Models (LLM), Knowledge Graphs (KG), and Bayesian Networks (BN). First, an LLM is utilized to automatically extract entities and causal relationships from heterogeneous texts to construct a structured fault KG. Second, the KG is mapped to a BN to realize dynamic probability reasoning under uncertainty, effectively distinguishing between “warning scenarios” and “self-healing scenarios”. Finally, a risk quantification mechanism based on Point-Biserial Correlation is introduced to generate graded warning levels. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves a fault pre-identification accuracy of 91.43% and an overall warning accuracy of 87.14%, outperforming traditional machine learning methods in timeliness and interpretability.
