AB  - Bridge inspection is a safety-critical task, yet its automation remains fragmented: existing methods address defect detection, defect quantification, or knowledge-based condition assessment in isolation, and no end-to-end pipeline translates raw defect imagery into standards-grounded maintenance decisions. To close this gap, we propose BRIDGEVIEW, an integrated framework that unifies computer vision, ontological reasoning, and Large Language Model (LLM)-based generation across two tightly coupled stages. The first stage performs defect detection and multi-measurement quantification: we introduce CoordYOLO, an enhanced You Only Look Once (YOLO) detector built on the YOLOv8s model, combining Coordinate Attention, an additional high-resolution detection head, and an attention-preserving pruning strategy for localizing subtle defects, coupled with algorithms that extract crack width, spallation area, and spallation depth. The second stage performs maintenance suggestion generation: a 1107-axiom bridge inspection ontology with Semantic Web Rule Language (SWRL) rules supplies standards-grounded reasoning that constrains Bridge-MT, a fine-tuned multi-task LLM that jointly classifies component condition states and generates maintenance recommendations. To support training and evaluation, we contribute two domain-specific datasets: 1358 annotated defect images and 14,754 prompt-response pairs derived from real-world inspection reports. CoordYOLO attains 85% mean Average Precision (mAP50) at 74 frames per second (FPS), while Bridge-MT reaches 87% component condition classification accuracy and surpasses comparably sized language models in maintenance-suggestion quality.
