AB  - Zero-shot anomaly detection (ZSAD) requires detection models trained on auxiliary data to identify anomalies without any training samples in the target dataset. This task is particularly important when data collection is limited by privacy or accessibility concerns, yet highly challenging since the models must generalize across diverse domains where object appearance, abnormal regions, and background features vary significantly. Recently, large pre-trained vision–language models (VLMs) such as CLIP have shown strong zero-shot recognition ability, but their ZSAD performance remains limited because they emphasize object semantics rather than normality/abnormality cues. In this paper, we introduce Adaptive Unified Prompt Anomaly Detection (AUPAD), the first one-for-all ZSAD framework that leverages the zero-shot recognition ability of VLMs through instance-adaptive prompt learning. Unlike prior methods with fixed category-specific prompts, AUPAD generates adaptive prompts by combining common semantic vectors with instance-adaptive tokens, enabling precise modeling of both normal and abnormal states in a category-guided manner. Furthermore, AUPAD incorporates an object-agnostic prompt template and a glocal abnormality loss to capture generic anomaly semantics from auxiliary data, while a Dynamic Region Attention Module (DRAM) alleviates global attention bias and enhances local semantic consistency without modifying the pretrained encoder. Extensive experiments on 17 industrial and medical datasets demonstrate that AUPAD achieves superior performance in detecting and segmenting anomalies across highly diverse semantic categories. Code will be made available at https://github.com/hanli6688/AUPAD.
