AB  - Building damage captioning is an emerging task within damage extraction, playing a crucial role in damage assessment and decision-making tasks. However, the complexity of disaster-affected environments and the diversity of damage manifestations make it challenging for current models to accurately identify damage features and generate comprehensive captions. To advance the development of building damage captioning and improve caption accuracy, this study constructed a dataset namedBD-CCand proposed a building damage feature keyword-guided caption model (KGBDCNet). In terms of dataset, the BD-CC dataset focuses on building damage in the Mariupol region, collecting a total of 4300 pairs of high-resolution RGB dual-temporal remote sensing images obtained from Google Earth. This includes 2877 pairs of images showing damaged buildings and 1423 pairs showing undamaged buildings, with all damage resulting from human conflict. Regarding the model, this paper utilizedLoRAto fine-tune theRemote-CLIPfor efficient extraction multi-modal features of damaged buildings. To fuse multi-modal features, this paper designed thecross-modal attention guided module (CAGM), which employs aretrieve-enhance strategyto guide damage keywords to enhance the visual feature representation of damage information. This paper further proposed thespatial–temporal memory-enhanced module (STMEM), which significantly improves the model’s recognition accuracy for changed regions and its ability to capture temporal evolution by establishing the dynamic associations of spatial–temporal features between bi-temporal images. The benchmarked experiment of the BD-CC dataset was conducted using current state-of-the-art methods to validate its effectiveness. On the public datasetLEVIR-CCand the self-built datasetBD-CC, comparison experiments were conducted betweenKGBDCNetand other methodsto test the model’s advancement. Additionally, we integrated alarge language modeland damaged grade judgement criteria to achievezero-shot building damage assessmentbased on the captions generated by the model, testing itsrobustness and generalizability. In the aforementioned experiments, the proposed method achieved superior results, demonstrating its effectiveness. The code of the proposed method will be made available at https://github.com/Lucky-DW/KGBDCNet.
