AB  - The escalating volume of literature demands automated knowledge extraction for autonomous robotic experimentation. This review traces the evolution from traditional rule-based and machine-learning pipelines to transformer-based large language models and multimodal architectures, encompassing domain-specific encoders and visual foundation models. We identify three persistent challenges: semantic complexity, multimodal heterogeneity, and rapid terminology evolution. We demonstrate that multimodal large language models, through context-aware reasoning, few-shot adaptation, and native cross-modal alignment, can systematically address these challenges, enabling holistic document-level understanding. Strategies including prompt engineering, retrieval-augmented generation, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning further enhance extraction reliability. ChemRobot systems can build structured actionable knowledge bases from unstructured texts, tables, and figures, closing the loop from literature to autonomous experimentation. We conclude with a roadmap for developing intelligent Literature Readers, emphasizing multimodal benchmarks, efficient architectures, uncertainty quantification, and human-machine collaboration.
