AB  - The prediction of biochemical process indicators is a fundamental task in AI4Biochemical, as accurate forecasting improves process transparency and decision-making efficiency. Conventional methods often treat this as a purely data-driven multivariate time series forecasting problem, overlooking the domain-specific insights from mechanistic models. However, relying solely on either mechanistic or time series models limits automation and generalization. To bridge this gap, we propose LMT-Net, a hybrid framework that integrates Large Language Models (LLMs), mechanistic models, and time series models. A key innovation of our framework is the strategic use of the LLM as a mechanistic inference agent to generate a relational graph, rather than as a direct numerical predictor. This design decouples knowledge extraction from prediction and, when paired with our proposed mechanistic router spatial attention block, creates an inherently robust system. This synergy ensures that even if the LLM hallucinates, the data-driven attention mechanism can learn to correct for flawed priors, a capability we validate through simulation experiments. Extensive experiments on a real-world biochemical dataset demonstrate that LMT-Net significantly outperforms state-of-the-art models in prediction accuracy. Furthermore, our analysis confirms the framework’s practical feasibility, showing that the LLM agent’s one-time inference cost is minimal in both time and budget, overcoming a major barrier for industrial deployment. This combination of accuracy, robustness, and cost-effectiveness establishes LMT-Net as a novel and viable paradigm for integrating LLMs into industrial systems.
