AB  - The operational integrity of fire service installations is crucial for infrastructure safety, sustainability and resilience. Traditional fire service inspection practices, relying on specific engineering knowledge and a deep understanding of fire provisions, are high-cost, labor-intensive and inconsistent due to human errors. General-purpose vision-language models lack domain-specific expertise and often hallucinate on regulatory details. This study proposes a domain-specialized multimodal framework, Qwen-Fire, to support human-in-the-loop and interactive fire service inspection and compliance verification in Hong Kong. A domain dataset is derived from official fire codes, followed by a parameter-efficient fine-tuning process to efficiently embed professional knowledge into the Qwen-Fire engine. Results show that Qwen-Fire achieved a superior recall rate of 0.757 and an F1 score of 0.648 against GPT-5.1 and DeepSeek-V3, validating its effectiveness in generating accurate regulations and audit suggestions for intelligent facility management. This framework integrates a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) mechanism that enables the precise retrieval of historical records, updated regulations, and physical sensor data. The RAG-enhanced framework outperforms hyperscale generalist models in domain-specific retrieval and reasoning tasks, with a factuality score of 0.616, a hallucination-free score of 0.884, and a citation score of 0.652. The localized deployment strategy provides a cost-effective and privacy-preserving foundational engine for transitioning building maintenance from passive manual checks to data-driven safety management.
