AB  - With the rise of Artificial Intelligence-Generated Content (AIGC), applications with fixed-pattern queries, such as those in marketing and education, can reduce interactions with Large Language Models (LLMs) via a set of ordered and well-defined prompts, referred to as a prompt chain. However, in such paradigms, a compromised prompt engineering server could quickly propagate malware and degrade AIGC performance across the network. To mitigate this risk, secure cooperation among distributed servers is crucial. In this work, we incorporate the Zero Trust (ZT) network access to secure server cooperation and explore cost-effective prompt chain deployment in ZT-enabled networks. We formalize the problem of Prompt Chain Deployment in ZT-enabled networks (PCD-ZT), aiming to minimize the total service cost, including deployment cost and ZT verification cost. To address PCD-ZT, we propose the Trust-pArtite Graph (TAG) to identify total service cost through various ZT verification modes and develop the TAG-based Prompt chAin Deployment (TAGPAD) algorithm. We prove the optimality of TAGPAD through mathematical analysis when sufficient resources are provided to accommodate the AIGC service. Extensive simulations address that TAGPAD outperforms existing benchmarks that are extended from the state-of-the-art regarding the total service and ZT verification cost.
