AB  - Interactive narrative systems for film and television require robust modeling of heterogeneous audiovisual and textual signals to support coherent, emotionally consistent, and real-time user interaction. However, existing approaches often rely on text-centric generation or shallow multimodal fusion, which limits their ability to capture scene-level semantics, narrative rhythm, and affective cues inherent in cinematic content. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a Scene-Aware Multimodal Transformer (SAMT) for controllable interactive narrative generation. The proposed framework adopts an end-to-end soft computing paradigm and integrates three key components: (1) a cross-modal alignment module based on contrastive learning to mitigate distributional discrepancies among video, audio, and text modalities; (2) a scene-aware fusion module with hierarchical cross-modal attention to model spatiotemporal dependencies and contextual audiovisual semantics; and (3) a contextual generation module that introduces explicit emotion and rhythm constraints to regulate narrative tone and pacing. In addition, a multimodal interactive film-fragment dataset is constructed to support systematic evaluation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SAMT consistently outperforms representative multimodal baselines and large-scale multimodal language models in terms of text generation quality, multimodal consistency, scene coherence, and user engagement, while maintaining low inference latency suitable for real-time interaction. These results indicate that soft alignment, scene-aware fusion, and controllable generation are critical for effective multimodal narrative modeling in complex interactive systems.
