AB  - Recent advancements in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in general visual reasoning, yet their applicability to rigorous forensic tasks such as handwriting movement analysis remains largely unexplored. This work presents a comprehensive study evaluating the zero-shot performance of state-of-the-art VLMs (GPT-5.2 and Gemini 2.5 Pro) on the Signature Verification Challenge (SVC) benchmark. To bridge the gap between static imagery and dynamic execution, we propose a preprocessing framework that encodes handwriting pressure dynamics into grayscale stroke intensity. Furthermore, we implement a dual-scoring protocol that extracts both latent token probabilities and self-reported certainty scores to evaluate biometric reliability. Experimental results across 45,520 evaluations reveal a significant performance dichotomy: in random forgery scenarios, zero-shot VLMs achieve exceptional discrimination, with GPT-5.2 reaching an Equal Error Rate (EER) of 0.21%, outperforming specialized supervised systems. Conversely, in skilled forgery scenarios, susceptibility to ungrounded linguistic rationalization emerges, where the text-generation layer triggers kinematic hallucinations, justifying forgery artifacts as natural variability. Our linguistic audit quantifies this phenomenon through the Kinematic Hallucination Index (KHI) and reveals a higher adjective density in GPT-5.2 (27.39%) compared to the more technical register of Gemini 2.5 Pro-(19.65%). Finally, we demonstrate that while CoT rarely flips categorical verdicts, it serves as a robust internal calibrator, with probabilistic shifts of over 50% perfectly correlating with forensic decision accuracy.
